 page=dict(
  sum="Doxycycline is a tetracycline antibiotic, not a peptide. It jams the machine bacteria use to build proteins, so they stop growing, and it gets inside cells where chlamydia hides. At low doses it also calms inflammation, which is why dermatologists use it for acne and rosacea.",
  deep="Doxycycline binds the 30S subunit of the bacterial ribosome and blocks transfer RNA from docking, so the bacterium cannot add amino acids to new proteins. This stops growth rather than killing outright (bacteriostatic), and the immune system clears what is left. It is fat-soluble, so it enters cells and tissues well, including the cervix, lungs, skin oil glands and prostate. Separately from its antibiotic action, it inhibits matrix metalloproteinases, enzymes that break down collagen during inflammation; this is the basis of 20 mg and 40 mg doses for gum disease and rosacea. Calcium, magnesium, iron, zinc and aluminium bind it in the gut and block absorption.",
  helps="First-choice treatment for chlamydia (100% cure in a 2015 NEJM trial), part of every PID regimen, standard for acne and rosacea, and used for Lyme disease, pneumonia, malaria prevention and tick-borne fevers. As doxy PEP, 200 mg after sex cut syphilis and chlamydia by more than 70% in high-risk groups.",
  tested="Large trials in chlamydia (vs azithromycin), doxy PEP (three randomised trials), acne, rosacea and periodontitis at sub-antibiotic doses, Lyme disease and malaria prophylaxis.",
  worked="Chlamydia cure near 100%. Rosacea reviews score 8 out of 10. Doxy PEP sharply cut new STIs in trials.",
  didnt="Sun sensitivity, nausea and food-pipe irritation make courses hard. Doxy PEP cuts gonorrhoea only about half, and resistance is the open worry. Calcium and iron in the same window quietly lower the dose you absorb.",
  reports="Drugs.com users give it 6.4 out of 10 across 1,659 reviews. Rosacea rates best (8.0), acne 6.7 (776 reviews), chlamydia 6.5. Nausea when taken on an empty stomach, sunburn and heartburn are the usual complaints.",
  found="A semi-synthetic tetracycline made by Pfizer in the 1960s from oxytetracycline, a soil-bacteria antibiotic. Not a peptide.",
  first="A broad antibiotic for chest, urinary and skin infections that needed fewer doses than the older tetracyclines.",
  story="Born as a longer-lasting tetracycline, it became the go-to for chlamydia, acne and travel malaria, and in 2024 a pill taken after sex to prevent STIs.",
  sources=S('dc_doxy','geisler','cdc21','cdc21b','pid14','doxypep','doxy_pdr','doxy_tips','probio','doxy_wiki'),
  pools=[["1,659","Drugs.com reviews; acne 776, bacterial infection 218, rosacea 115, chlamydia 101, lyme disease 73",1],["6.4/10","average rating, 49% positive, 27% negative",1],["8.0/10","rosacea, best rated use (115 reviews)",1]],
  wk=["Rosacea rated 8.0 out of 10 (115 reviews)","Chlamydia cleared in a week","Acne calms over 6 to 12 weeks for many"],
  dn=["Bad sunburn after short sun","Nausea or vomiting on an empty stomach","Reflux or painful swallowing"],
  rp=["Nausea, worse without food","Sun sensitivity and burns","Acid reflux, pain when swallowing","Upset stomach and diarrhoea","Vaginal yeast infection after the course"],
  originDetail="Not a peptide · semi-synthetic tetracycline",
  dose={"tiers":[
    {"label":"Chlamydia, cervicitis","dose":"100 mg","freq":"2× daily","dur":"7 days","route":"Oral","who":"CDC 2021 first choice","tag":"verified","src":S('cdc21b')},
    {"label":"PID (with metronidazole)","dose":"100 mg","freq":"2× daily","dur":"14 days","route":"Oral","who":"with ceftriaxone + metronidazole","tag":"verified","src":S('pid14')},
    {"label":"Doxy PEP","dose":"200 mg","freq":"once, within 72 h of sex","dur":"max 200 mg per 24 h","route":"Oral","who":"CDC 2024, higher-risk groups","tag":"verified","src":S('doxypep')},
    {"label":"Acne, rosacea","dose":"40–100 mg","freq":"1–2× daily","dur":"6–12 wk or longer","route":"Oral","who":"dermatology","tag":"verified","src":S('doxy_wiki')}],
   "checked":["labels","CDC 2021, 2024","reviews"],"src":S('cdc21b')},
  mx=["Bacteria build proteins on ribosomes, reading genetic code and adding one amino acid at a time. Doxycycline sits on the small (30S) half of the bacterial ribosome and stops the next amino acid from docking. Without new proteins the bacterium cannot grow or divide, and your immune system finishes the job.",
      "Chlamydia lives inside your own cells, where many antibiotics cannot reach. Doxycycline is fat-soluble and enters cells easily, which is why it beats single-dose azithromycin on cure and is first choice for chlamydia, cervicitis and the chlamydia side of PID.",
      "In acne and rosacea, part of the effect is not about killing bacteria. Doxycycline calms the inflammatory signals in oil glands and skin vessels, which is why a 40 mg dose too low to act as an antibiotic still clears rosacea bumps.",
      "Matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) are enzymes that cut collagen during inflammation, in gums, skin and joints. Doxycycline blocks them, so 20 mg twice daily slows gum disease. This is the same tissue-protecting idea some readers know from repair peptides, through a different door."],
  sx={"Metronidazole":"The two halves of PID treatment: doxycycline covers chlamydia and oxygen-tolerant bacteria, metronidazole the anaerobes. CDC 2021 gives both for 14 days with a ceftriaxone shot. No interaction between them.",
      "Dexketoprofen":"Pain relief while the antibiotic works. No known interaction. Both are kinder to the stomach with food (keep dairy away from the doxycycline).",
      "Tranexamic acid":"Often in the same prescription when infection comes with bleeding. No known interaction."},
  hasDeep=True,
 ),
 card="Some bacteria hide inside your own cells, where most antibiotics cannot follow. Doxycycline slips into cells and jams the machine bacteria use to build proteins, so they stop growing. In a 2015 trial it cured 100% of chlamydia, against 97% for single-dose azithromycin, and since 2024 a 200 mg dose after sex is used to prevent STIs. The plain truth: calcium, iron and magnesium within 2 to 3 hours steal part of the dose, and the sun can burn you in minutes while you take it.",
 punch="The antibiotic that follows bacteria inside cells. Chlamydia, acne, rosacea; keep minerals and midday sun away.",
 take={"what":"A tetracycline antibiotic that stops bacteria building proteins and calms skin inflammation at low dose.",
       "legal":"Prescription medicine everywhere, FDA approved since 1967 and on the WHO essential list. CDC first choice for chlamydia and the doxy PEP pill.",
       "use":"Chlamydia, PID (with metronidazole), acne, rosacea, chest infections, Lyme disease, malaria prevention, STI prevention after sex.",
       "ev":"Strong trial record: 100% chlamydia cure in NEJM 2015; doxy PEP cut syphilis and chlamydia by over 70%. Gonorrhoea protection is only about half, and resistance is being watched."},
 faq=[["Why can't I take it with milk or my magnesium?","Calcium, magnesium, iron, zinc and aluminium latch onto doxycycline in the gut and stop it being absorbed. Keep dairy, antacids, multivitamins and mineral supplements 2 to 3 hours away from each dose. Doxycycline binds calcium less than older tetracyclines, but the gap is still the label advice."],
      ["How bad is the sun on it?","Bad enough to plan around. It can cause an exaggerated sunburn within minutes to hours, sometimes with blisters or nail lifting. In strong tropical sun: cover up, SPF 30+ reapplied, avoid the middle of the day, and remember it lasts a few days after the last pill."],
      ["Why stay upright after the pill?","A capsule that sticks in the food pipe can burn an ulcer, even after one dose. Swallow it with a full glass of water, sitting or standing, and stay upright at least 30 minutes. Never take it right before bed."],
      ["Doxycycline or azithromycin for chlamydia?","Doxycycline cured 100% vs 97% for azithromycin in the 2015 NEJM trial, so CDC made 7 days of doxycycline first choice in 2021. Azithromycin wins on convenience (one dose) and in pregnancy."],
      ["Can I take it with my peptides?","No known interaction with BPC-157, TB-500, KPV, NAD+ or brain peptides. GHK-Cu carries copper, a metal doxycycline binds, but injected GHK-Cu never meets the pill in the gut, so no real clash is expected. With a GLP-1, the nausea adds up, and slower stomach emptying plus reflux is one more reason to take doxycycline upright with lots of water."],
      ["Does it stop the pill working?","The label still mentions it, but studies have not shown that doxycycline lowers contraceptive hormone levels the way rifampicin does. Vomiting or diarrhoea can, so use backup if that happens."]],
 deep={"asof":"2026-11","sections":[
   {"h":"Why it became first choice for chlamydia","where":"people","body":[
    "For years chlamydia was treated with either a single gram of azithromycin or a week of doxycycline. A 2015 randomised trial in 567 young people in Los Angeles youth facilities, where re-exposure could be controlled, found doxycycline cured 100% and azithromycin 97%, so azithromycin could not be called equivalent [1] (verified).",
    "The CDC changed its guideline in 2021: doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days is now the recommended chlamydia treatment, with azithromycin as the alternative, mainly in pregnancy [2][3]. The same doxycycline dose for 14 days is part of every PID regimen, alongside metronidazole [4]."]},
   {"h":"Doxy PEP: an antibiotic as prevention","where":"people","body":[
    "In June 2024 the CDC recommended doxycycline post-exposure prophylaxis: 200 mg taken within 72 hours of condomless sex, at most 200 mg in 24 hours, for men who have sex with men and transgender women with a recent bacterial STI [5]. Across three randomised trials it cut syphilis and chlamydia by more than 70% and gonorrhoea by about half [5] (verified).",
    "The open question is resistance, in gonorrhoea and in the background bacteria of the gut and skin. CDC pairs it with STI testing every 3 to 6 months. Trials in cisgender women have not shown the same benefit (reported)."]},
   {"h":"The two absorption traps: minerals and the food pipe","where":"people","body":[
    "Doxycycline chelates divalent and trivalent metals. The label lists aluminium, calcium and magnesium antacids, iron supplements, bismuth and multivitamins with zinc or manganese as reducing absorption [6]. The usual practice is 2 to 3 hours between doxycycline and any of these [7] (verified).",
    "The second trap is mechanical. Tetracycline-class capsules can lodge in the oesophagus and cause ulcers, including after a single dose; the label advises plenty of fluid and not taking it at bedtime [6][7]. Taking it with a meal (not dairy) reduces nausea without a meaningful loss of absorption (reported)."]},
   {"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":[
    "How much doxy PEP will drive resistance at population scale. Whether its MMP-blocking effect matters for tendon and joint tissue in people, a question that overlaps with what repair peptides are used for; human data are limited to gum disease and rosacea (inferred). Whether any peptide changes its absorption: none is known to."]}],
  "refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Geisler et al., NEJM","url":SRC['geisler'][1],"year":"2015"},{"n":2,"t":"CDC STI Treatment Guidelines 2021, summary","url":SRC['cdc21'][1],"year":"2021"},{"n":3,"t":"CDC 2021 vs 2015 comparison","url":SRC['cdc21b'][1]},{"n":4,"t":"PID regimen (5-Minute Clinical Consult)","url":SRC['pid14'][1]},{"n":5,"t":"CDC doxy PEP guidelines","url":SRC['doxypep'][1],"year":"2024"},{"n":6,"t":"Doxycycline prescribing summary (PDR)","url":SRC['doxy_pdr'][1]},{"n":7,"t":"Doxycycline prescribing tips (MedNext)","url":SRC['doxy_tips'][1],"year":"2025"}]},
