 page=dict(
  sum="Tranexamic acid is a small synthetic molecule, a copy of part of the amino acid lysine, not a peptide. It blocks the enzyme that dissolves blood clots, so clots that have already formed hold for longer. That is why it cuts heavy period bleeding, nosebleeds and surgical bleeding without making the blood thicker everywhere.",
  deep="When a blood vessel is damaged, the body forms a fibrin clot and, at the same time, starts plasminogen activation, which turns plasminogen into plasmin, the enzyme that dissolves fibrin. Tranexamic acid occupies the lysine-binding sites on plasminogen, so it cannot attach to fibrin and the clot is not broken down early. The womb lining is rich in plasminogen activators, which is why period bleeding responds well. It is excreted unchanged by the kidneys, half-life around 2 hours.",
  helps="Reduces heavy menstrual blood loss more than NSAIDs (Cochrane). In 20,000 trauma patients (CRASH-2) and 20,000 women bleeding after birth (WOMAN) it reduced deaths from bleeding. Oral low doses lighten melasma in small trials.",
  tested="Placebo-controlled trials for heavy menstrual bleeding (Lysteda), huge trials in trauma (CRASH-2), childbirth (WOMAN), head injury (CRASH-3) and gut bleeding (HALT-IT), plus melasma trials.",
  worked="Heavy periods: users report flow down sharply, sometimes within the first doses. Trauma and childbirth: fewer bleeding deaths when given early.",
  didnt="In gut bleeding (HALT-IT) it saved no lives and doubled venous clots at a high 24-hour IV dose. In melasma the pigment tends to return after stopping.",
  reports="Drugs.com users give it 7.0 out of 10 across 285 reviews, 60% positive. Heavy menstrual bleeding scores 7.5 (130 reviews), melasma 5.7. Headache, dizziness, nasal stuffiness and spotting are the usual complaints.",
  found="A synthetic lysine look-alike discovered in Japan in 1962 by Utako and Shosuke Okamoto. Not a peptide.",
  first="Bleeding after childbirth was Okamoto's goal; it was first approved for dental extractions in people with haemophilia.",
  story="A cheap molecule a Japanese researcher hoped would save mothers. It took 55 years and a 20,000-woman trial, and it became a staple in trauma bays and for heavy periods.",
  sources=S('dc_txa','lysteda','lysteda2','cochrane_hmb','cochrane_pan','crash2','haltit','txa_oral','txa_wiki'),
  pools=[["285","Drugs.com reviews; heavy menstrual bleeding 130, menstrual disorders 109, bleeding disorder 25, melasma 18",1],["7.0/10","average rating, 60% positive, 23% negative",1],["7.5/10","heavy menstrual bleeding (130 reviews)",1]],
  wk=["Flow down 50 to 80% in many reviews","Some feel it within the first doses","Fewer leaks and night changes"],
  dn=["Little effect for some","Melasma reviews only 5.7 out of 10","Big tablets, hard to swallow"],
  rp=["Lighter periods","Headache","Dizziness","Nasal or sinus stuffiness","Spotting between periods"],
  originDetail="Not a peptide · synthetic lysine analogue",
  dose={"tiers":[
    {"label":"Heavy periods (US label)","dose":"1,300 mg","freq":"3× daily","dur":"up to 5 days of the period","route":"Oral","who":"Lysteda, 2 × 650 mg","tag":"verified","src":S('lysteda')},
    {"label":"500 mg tablets","dose":"500–1,000 mg","freq":"2–3× daily","dur":"bleeding days","route":"Oral","who":"Kalnex, Transamin style tablets","tag":"reported","src":S('txa_wiki')},
    {"label":"Melasma (off label)","dose":"250 mg","freq":"2× daily","dur":"8–12 wk","route":"Oral","who":"dermatology trials","tag":"reported","src":S('txa_oral')}],
   "checked":["labels","trials","reviews"],"src":S('lysteda')},
  mx=["Clots are always being made and dissolved. The dissolving enzyme, plasmin, comes from plasminogen, which grabs fibrin through lysine-binding sites. Tranexamic acid fills those sites, so plasminogen cannot grab the clot. The clot is left to do its job.",
      "The womb lining releases lots of plasminogen activator during a period, which dissolves clots fast and keeps blood flowing. Damping that lets the small clots in the lining hold, so less blood is lost, only on the days you take it.",
      "Plasmin also feeds signals (prostaglandins and others) that push pigment cells to make more melanin. Blocking plasmin quietens that signal, which is the leading explanation for its melasma effect.",
      "It does not switch on clotting or thicken blood; it only slows the breakdown of clots already there. That is why pooled trials show low clot rates, but also why it is avoided in people already prone to clots, or on hormones that raise clot risk."],
  sx={"Dexketoprofen":"Two separate angles on a heavy, painful period: tranexamic acid lightens the flow, an NSAID eases cramps and also trims blood loss a little. Often prescribed together. No interaction.",
      "Metronidazole":"Sometimes in the same prescription when bleeding comes with an infection. No known interaction.",
      "Doxycycline":"Sometimes in the same prescription when bleeding comes with an infection. No known interaction."},
  hasDeep=True,
 ),
 card="During a period the womb lining releases an enzyme that dissolves clots, so blood keeps flowing. Tranexamic acid blocks the scissors that cut clots apart, so the small clots already there hold, and flow drops. It cuts heavy menstrual bleeding more than NSAIDs do, and in 20,000 trauma patients given it early, fewer died from bleeding. The plain truth: it is taken only on bleeding days, and never with the combined pill or a clot history.",
 punch="Keeps clots from dissolving too soon. Heavy periods, nosebleeds, melasma; never with the combined pill.",
 take={"what":"A synthetic lysine look-alike that stops clots from being dissolved too early.",
       "legal":"Prescription medicine (pharmacist-sold in some countries), FDA approved for heavy periods (Lysteda) and as an injection; WHO essential medicine.",
       "use":"Heavy periods, nosebleeds, bleeding after dental work or surgery, trauma care, and off-label pills for melasma.",
       "ev":"Big trials: fewer bleeding deaths in trauma and after childbirth; more effective than NSAIDs for heavy periods. No benefit in gut bleeding, where high IV doses raised clots."},
 faq=[["Does it cause blood clots?","It does not create clots; it protects ones already formed. In more than 2,500 people in oral TXA trials, no clot events were seen, and in CRASH-2 and WOMAN clot events were not higher. The risk shows up when other clot risks stack: the combined pill, a past clot, smoking, or a high IV dose (HALT-IT). That is why the label bans the combined pill with it."],
      ["How fast does it work?","Many users notice lighter flow on the first or second day. The label says to judge it over 2 cycles before deciding it does not work."],
      ["Do I take it all month?","No. Only on bleeding days, up to 5 days per period on the US label."],
      ["Tranexamic acid or an NSAID for heavy periods?","Cochrane: NSAIDs reduce blood loss compared with nothing, but less than tranexamic acid. NSAIDs also ease cramps, which TXA does not. Many people use both."],
      ["Can I take it with my peptides?","No known interaction with BPC-157, TB-500, GHK-Cu, GLP-1s or the rest; none are known to change clot breakdown in people. Hormone-type compounds are the ones to check: anything raising oestrogen adds clot risk."],
      ["Does it work for melasma?","Small trials of 250 mg twice daily lighten melasma over 8 to 12 weeks, often with sunscreen and a cream. Pigment tends to return after stopping, and Drugs.com reviewers rate it only 5.7 out of 10 for melasma."]],
 deep={"asof":"2026-11","sections":[
   {"h":"Heavy periods: how it stacks up","where":"people","body":[
    "Tranexamic acid is FDA approved for cyclic heavy menstrual bleeding as Lysteda, 1,300 mg three times daily for up to 5 days, after other causes such as polyps or fibroids are checked [1][2] (verified).",
    "A Cochrane review of 19 trials concluded that NSAIDs reduce heavy bleeding compared with placebo but are less effective than tranexamic acid or a hormonal IUD [3]. A Cochrane overview rated the evidence for tranexamic acid against placebo as moderate certainty [4] (verified). In practice many people pair it with an NSAID: the NSAID handles cramps, tranexamic acid the volume."]},
   {"h":"The big trials and the clot question","where":"people","body":[
    "CRASH-2 (about 20,000 trauma patients) and WOMAN (about 20,000 women bleeding after birth) both found fewer deaths from bleeding, and pooled clot events were not higher on tranexamic acid: 1.0% against 1.2% on placebo [5] (verified).",
    "HALT-IT is the counterweight. In 12,009 people with gut bleeding, a high 24-hour IV dose did not reduce deaths and nearly doubled venous clots (0.8% vs 0.4%) [6]. A 2025 review of oral TXA trials found no clot events in more than 2,500 participants treated for up to 24 months [7] (reported). Read together: low oral doses for a few days look safe in people without clot risks, while high IV doses in very sick people are a different situation."]},
   {"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":[
    "Long-term oral use for melasma, months at a time, has less safety data than period use. Whether peptides that raise blood vessel growth (BPC-157, TB-500) interact with clot breakdown in people is unstudied (inferred)."]}],
  "refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Lysteda US label","url":SRC['lysteda'][1],"year":"2009"},{"n":2,"t":"Lysteda prescribing information","url":SRC['lysteda2'][1]},{"n":3,"t":"Cochrane: NSAIDs for heavy menstrual bleeding","url":SRC['cochrane_hmb'][1],"year":"2019"},{"n":4,"t":"Cochrane overview, heavy menstrual bleeding","url":SRC['cochrane_pan'][1]},{"n":5,"t":"CRASH-2 and WOMAN pooled table","url":SRC['crash2'][1]},{"n":6,"t":"HALT-IT, The Lancet","url":SRC['haltit'][1],"year":"2020"},{"n":7,"t":"Oral TXA RCT review","url":SRC['txa_oral'][1],"year":"2025"}]},
