 page=dict(
  sum="Dexketoprofen is an NSAID painkiller, the active half of ketoprofen, not a peptide. It turns down prostaglandins, the signals that make injured tissue hurt and swell and make the womb cramp, and it reaches the blood within 30 minutes, so it is used for sharp, short-lived pain: period cramps, teeth, sprains and after surgery.",
  deep="Prostaglandins are made by two enzymes, COX-1 and COX-2. COX-2 rises at sites of injury and inflammation and drives pain and swelling; COX-1 keeps the stomach lining, kidney blood flow and platelets working. Dexketoprofen blocks both. As the trometamol salt it dissolves and absorbs quickly, with peak blood levels in 15 to 45 minutes and a half-life around 1.65 hours, hence dosing every 8 hours. Blocking COX-1 is the source of its stomach, bleeding and kidney risks.",
  helps="Single doses relieve acute pain as well as other NSAIDs at a lower dose, with faster onset: after wisdom tooth removal it halved pain in 0.9 hours versus 2.1 for ibuprofen. It eases period pain and reduces opioid needs after surgery.",
  tested="Randomised trials in dental, postoperative, back and musculoskeletal pain, renal colic and period pain; a 2008 systematic review and a 2019 narrative review.",
  worked="Fast relief within about 30 minutes; comparable to ibuprofen 400 mg or diclofenac 50 mg.",
  didnt="Pain relief plateaus at 25 mg, so higher doses add risk, not relief. Short half-life means pain returns between doses. Not studied for long-term use.",
  reports="No large user-review dataset found: dexketoprofen is not sold in the US, so Drugs.com and WebMD have no page. Indonesian and Spanish pharmacy pages describe it for cramps, toothache and sprains; stomach upset is the main complaint named.",
  found="Developed by Menarini in the 1990s by isolating the active mirror-image form of ketoprofen. Not a peptide.",
  first="Acute pain of mild to moderate strength: musculoskeletal pain, period pain and toothache.",
  story="Half of an old painkiller, separated out so the same relief comes from half the dose and faster.",
  sources=S('dex_pk','dex_pk2','dex_rev','dex_tf','dex_dys','ketesse_id','ketesse_mims','nsaid_tendon','nsaid_tendon2','nsaid_tendon3','bpc_nsaid','dex_wiki'),
  pools=None,
  wk=["Relief in about 30 minutes","Lower dose than ibuprofen for the same effect","Reduces opioid needs after surgery"],
  dn=["Wears off in a few hours","Stomach upset","No benefit from going above 25 mg"],
  rp=["Fast relief of period cramps","Toothache and dental pain","Sprains and back pain","Stomach upset or reflux"],
  originDetail="Not a peptide · arylpropionic acid NSAID",
  dose={"tiers":[
    {"label":"Label","dose":"25 mg","freq":"every 8 h","dur":"short term, max 75 mg a day","route":"Oral","who":"acute pain","tag":"verified","src":S('ketesse_mims')},
    {"label":"Lower step","dose":"12.5 mg","freq":"every 4–6 h","dur":"short term","route":"Oral","who":"half tablet","tag":"verified","src":S('ketesse_id')},
    {"label":"Injection","dose":"50 mg","freq":"every 8–12 h","dur":"hospital, max 150 mg a day","route":"IM / IV","who":"after surgery","tag":"verified","src":S('dex_rev')}],
   "checked":["labels","reviews of trials"],"src":S('ketesse_mims')},
  mx=["When tissue is injured, COX enzymes turn fats in cell membranes into prostaglandins. These make nerve endings more sensitive and widen vessels so the area swells. Dexketoprofen blocks COX, so fewer prostaglandins are made and pain and swelling ease.",
      "Ketoprofen is a mix of two mirror-image molecules. Only the S form blocks COX well. Dexketoprofen is the S form alone, so half the dose gives the same effect, with less drug for the liver and kidneys to clear.",
      "The trometamol salt dissolves fast in the stomach. Peak blood levels arrive in 15 to 45 minutes, which is why it is chosen for sudden pain. Food slows this, so the label suggests 30 minutes before meals for fast relief, while many doctors say after meals to spare the stomach.",
      "Period cramps come from prostaglandins squeezing the womb muscle and narrowing its vessels. Blocking them eases the cramp and, as a side effect, slightly reduces bleeding.",
      "COX-1 keeps a protective mucus and bicarbonate layer in the stomach. Blocking it is the price of NSAIDs: heartburn, ulcers and, rarely, bleeding, more so with alcohol, steroids, blood thinners or long use."],
  sx={"Tranexamic acid":"Heavy, painful periods: dexketoprofen eases cramps, tranexamic acid cuts the flow. Often prescribed together; no interaction.",
      "Metronidazole":"Pain relief while an antibiotic treats a pelvic or dental infection. No known interaction; take both with food.",
      "Doxycycline":"Pain relief while an antibiotic works. No known interaction."},
  hasDeep=True,
 ),
 card="Cramps, toothache and sprains hurt because injured tissue makes prostaglandins, signals that sharpen pain and squeeze muscle. Dexketoprofen is the working half of the old painkiller ketoprofen, split out so it acts at half the dose and reaches the blood in about 30 minutes. After wisdom tooth removal it halved pain in 0.9 hours, against 2.1 for ibuprofen. The plain truth: it wears off within hours, it costs the stomach lining, and it may slow early tendon healing.",
 punch="The fast half of ketoprofen. Period cramps and toothache eased in about 30 minutes; short courses only.",
 take={"what":"An NSAID painkiller: the active half of ketoprofen, absorbed fast.",
       "legal":"Prescription medicine in Europe, the UK, Indonesia and much of Asia (Ketesse, Keral). Never approved in the US.",
       "use":"Short bursts of acute pain: period cramps, toothache, sprains, back pain, after surgery.",
       "ev":"Many randomised trials in acute pain: as strong as ibuprofen 400 mg at a lower dose and faster. No data for long-term use; stomach and kidney risks as for any NSAID."},
 faq=[["Before or after food?","The label says food slows absorption, so for fast relief take it 30 minutes before a meal. With food or after a meal is gentler on the stomach. If your pharmacist wrote after meals, follow that."],
      ["Can I take it with paracetamol?","Yes. They work differently and are commonly combined. Do not combine it with another NSAID (ibuprofen, naproxen, aspirin, diclofenac, mefenamic acid)."],
      ["Is it stronger than ibuprofen?","Not stronger, faster. 25 mg works like ibuprofen 400 mg, with relief in about 30 minutes against 1 to 2 hours. Ibuprofen lasts a little longer."],
      ["Will it slow my injury healing?","Possibly, early on. Animal studies show NSAIDs given right after a tendon or bone injury can weaken repair, while delayed use does not. Human data are mixed. For a fresh tendon injury you are treating with repair peptides, many prefer paracetamol in the first days (inferred)."],
      ["Can I take it with my peptides?","With BPC-157: no clash known; in rats BPC-157 protected the stomach from NSAID damage, but that is animal data only. With a GLP-1: watch hydration, because vomiting plus an NSAID strains the kidneys. With repair peptides generally: it may pull against early healing (see above)."],
      ["How long can I take it?","Short term only, the label says, limited to the symptomatic period. Pain that needs more than a few days needs a check."]],
 deep={"asof":"2026-11","sections":[
   {"h":"Why split a painkiller in half","where":"people","body":[
    "Ketoprofen is racemic: equal parts of two mirror-image forms. Only the S(+) form, dexketoprofen, strongly blocks COX. Dexketoprofen trometamol 12.5 and 25 mg give the same exposure as racemic ketoprofen 25 and 50 mg [1][2] (verified).",
    "The trometamol salt adds speed. Peak blood levels arrive in 15 to 45 minutes, and after wisdom tooth removal the time to halve pain was 0.9 hours, against 2.1 hours for ibuprofen [3] (verified). Relief plateaus at 25 mg, so the 75 mg daily ceiling is about safety, not missing benefit [1]."]},
   {"h":"How it compares in trials","where":"people","body":[
    "A 2019 review found single oral doses relieve acute pain within about 30 minutes, are well tolerated short term, and reduce opioid needs after surgery [4]. Efficacy at 25 mg is comparable to ibuprofen 400 mg and diclofenac 50 mg (reported).",
    "In a 2018 emergency room trial for period pain, IV dexketoprofen and IV paracetamol both reduced pain, with dexketoprofen numerically larger and fewer needing rescue medicine (1 of 49 vs 4 of 50), a difference that was not statistically significant [5]."]},
   {"h":"NSAIDs and healing: the question for repair-peptide users","where":"animals","body":[
    "Prostaglandins are part of the early repair signal. In rats, ibuprofen given right after a tendon repair weakened healing, while the same drug started later did not [6]. A review of bone, tendon and enthesis healing concluded animal data raise concern but human studies are few [7], and a 2019 meta-analysis found insufficient evidence for or against NSAIDs at the tendon-bone junction [8] (reported).",
    "In rats, BPC-157 given with indomethacin, aspirin or diclofenac reduced gut lesions [9] (animal only). No human study has tested an NSAID with any repair peptide."]},
   {"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":[
    "Whether a few days of an NSAID meaningfully slows tendon healing in people. Whether BPC-157's stomach protection seen in rats applies to people. How dexketoprofen compares with naproxen over a whole period."]}],
  "refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Barbanoj et al., clinical pharmacokinetics","url":SRC['dex_pk'][1],"year":"2001"},{"n":2,"t":"Mauleon et al.","url":SRC['dex_pk2'][1],"year":"1996"},{"n":3,"t":"Dexketoprofen pharmacology (Taylor and Francis)","url":SRC['dex_tf'][1]},{"n":4,"t":"Hanna and Moon, review","url":SRC['dex_rev'][1],"year":"2019"},{"n":5,"t":"Serinken et al., Balkan Med J","url":SRC['dex_dys'][1],"year":"2018"},{"n":6,"t":"Ibuprofen timing and tendon healing (rats)","url":SRC['nsaid_tendon2'][1]},{"n":7,"t":"Su and O'Connor, J Appl Physiol","url":SRC['nsaid_tendon'][1],"year":"2013"},{"n":8,"t":"Duchman et al., meta-analysis","url":SRC['nsaid_tendon3'][1],"year":"2019"},{"n":9,"t":"Sikiric et al., BPC-157 and NSAID lesions","url":SRC['bpc_nsaid'][1],"year":"1997"}]},
